<!-- ============ 1 · BASICS ============ -->
<section id="ch40-basics">
  <div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-no">01</div><div>
    <h2>What an aerosol is — and what "output" really means</h2>
    <p>The whole chapter stands on one definition. Get this, and every device after it is just a different way of making the same thing.</p>
    <p><span class="chip">Slides 3–4</span></p>
  </div></div>

  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="pop"><figure><img class="tilt" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Aerosol cloud of particles in gas"/>
    <figcaption><p class="anchor"><b>Memory anchor:</b> An aerosol is a suspension of solid or liquid particles in gas — and size decides where each particle lands: <span class="num rv">2–5 µm</span> reaches the lower airways, <span class="num rv">1–3 µm</span> the alveoli.</p></figcaption></figure></div>
    <div class="stack">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>💨 The definition (slide 3)</h3>
        <p><b>Aerosol:</b> a suspension of solid or liquid particles in gas. In the clinical setting, medical aerosols are generated with three kinds of devices:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Atomizers</b> convert liquid into droplets.</li>
          <li><b>Nebulizers</b> produce finer aerosol particles from liquid-based formulations over greater periods of time, using compressed gas or electricity.</li>
          <li><b>Inhalers</b> generate aerosolized medication from liquids or dry powders for a single inhalation.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><b>Aim of aerosol therapy:</b> deliver a therapeutic dose of the selected agent to the desired site of action. Medication aerosol gives a <b>higher therapeutic index</b> — higher local drug concentration in the lung, lower systemic effects.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>📤 Output, output rate, emitted dose (slide 4)</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Aerosol output:</b> the mass of fluid or drug contained in the aerosol.</li>
          <li><b>Output rate:</b> the mass of aerosol generated per unit of time — it varies with the nebulizer or inhaler used.</li>
          <li><b>Emitted dose:</b> the mass of drug leaving the mouthpiece as aerosol, measured by collecting the aerosol leaving the nebulizer on filters.</li>
          <li><b>Gravimetric analysis</b> measures aerosol weight; <b>assay</b> measures the quantity of drug.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
<nav class="prevnext" aria-label="Section navigation"><a href="#ch40-size" class="next">2 · Particle Size →</a></nav></section>

<!-- ============ 2 · PARTICLE SIZE ============ -->
<section id="ch40-size">
  <div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-no">02</div><div>
    <h2>Particle size: MMAD, VMD, GSD</h2>
    <p>Three letters that decide whether a dose works — and two different instruments that measure them.</p>
    <p><span class="chip">Slide 5</span></p>
  </div></div>

  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>📏 What size depends on</h3>
      <p>Particle size depends on three factors: the <b>substance being nebulized</b>, the <b>method used to generate the aerosol</b>, and <b>environmental conditions</b>.</p>
      <table>
        <tr><th>Measurement method</th><th>What it gives you</th></tr>
        <tr><td data-k="Method">Cascade impaction</td><td data-k="Gives"><b>MMAD</b> — mass median aerodynamic diameter</td></tr>
        <tr><td data-k="Method">Laser diffraction</td><td data-k="Gives"><b>VMD</b> — volume median diameter</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>🎲 Heterodisperse, monodisperse, GSD</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Heterodisperse:</b> particles of many different sizes.</li>
        <li><b>Monodisperse:</b> particles of similar sizes.</li>
        <li><b>GSD (geometric standard deviation)</b> describes the variability of particle sizes — the greater the GSD, the wider the range of particle sizes and the more heterodisperse the aerosol.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="anchor"><b>Memory anchor:</b> MMAD comes from cascade impaction, VMD from laser diffraction — and a big GSD means a wide, messy spread of sizes.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="lab" style="margin-top:16px">
    <h3>🔬 Try it: the particle-size explorer — pick a size, see where it lands</h3>
