<!-- ============ 3 · DEPOSITION ============ -->
<section id="ch40-deposition">
  <div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-no">03</div><div>
    <h2>Deposition: inhaled mass, respirable mass, and the three mechanisms</h2>
    <p>Only a fraction of what leaves the device ever reaches the lung — and three different physics tricks put it there.</p>
    <p><span class="chip">Slides 6–10</span></p>
  </div></div>

  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="pop"><figure><img class="tilt" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Three aerosol deposition mechanisms"/>
    <figcaption><p class="anchor"><b>Memory anchor:</b> Big particles crash (impaction, over <span class="num rv">5 µm</span>), medium ones settle (sedimentation, <span class="num rv">1–5 µm</span>), tiny ones wander (diffusion, under <span class="num rv">3 µm</span>) — three mechanisms, three addresses in the lung.</p></figcaption></figure></div>
    <div class="stack">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>📉 Emitted → inhaled → respirable (slide 6)</h3>
        <p>Only a fraction of the emitted aerosol (the <b>emitted dose</b>) will be inhaled. Only a fraction of what is inhaled (the <b>respirable dose</b>) is deposited in the lungs.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Inhaled mass:</b> the amount of drug inhaled.</li>
          <li><b>Respirable mass:</b> the portion of inhaled mass that can reach the lower airways.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><b>Deposition is influenced by</b> (slide 7): inspiratory flow rate, flow pattern, respiratory rate, inhaled volume, I:E ratio, and breath-holding.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>⚙️ The three mechanisms (slides 7–10)</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Inertial impaction:</b> aerosol in motion collides with and deposits on a surface. The primary mechanism for <b>larger particles (&gt;<span class="num rv">5 µm</span>)</b>. The greater the mass and velocity of a moving object, the greater the inertia and the tendency of that object to continue moving along its set path — so particles crash at bends instead of turning.</li>
          <li><b>Gravitational sedimentation:</b> particles settle out of suspension and deposit due to gravity. The primary mechanism for <b>small particles (<span class="num rv">1–5 µm</span>)</b>. Greater mass settles faster. <b>Breath-holding after inhalation increases sedimentation</b> and distribution across the lungs.</li>
          <li><b>Brownian diffusion:</b> the primary mechanism for <b>very small particles (&lt;<span class="num rv">3 µm</span>)</b> deep within the lung. Particles between <span class="num rv">1 and 0.5 µm</span> have very low mass and are so stable that most remain in suspension and are <b>exhaled</b> back into the environment. Particles <b>&lt;<span class="num rv">0.5 µm</span></b> have a greater retention rate in the lungs.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="hint">Note: slide 7 lists the three mechanisms as inertial impaction, "gravimetric" sedimentation, and Brownian diffusion — the deck's spelling; the mechanism is gravitational settling.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
