<!-- ============ 4 · TARGETS & AGING ============ -->
<section id="ch40-targets">
  <div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-no">04</div><div>
    <h2>Where particles land — and how aerosols age</h2>
    <p>The deck's target table has a quirk in it, taught here exactly as written. Don't let it trip you on the exam.</p>
    <p><span class="chip">Slides 11–12</span></p>
  </div></div>

  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>🎯 Recommended MMAD by target (slide 11)</h3>
      <table>
        <tr><th>Target</th><th>Recommended MMAD</th></tr>
        <tr><td data-k="Target">Upper airway (nose, larynx, trachea)</td><td data-k="MMAD"><span class="num rv">5 to &gt;50 µm</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td data-k="Target">Lower airways</td><td data-k="MMAD"><span class="num rv">2 to 5 µm</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td data-k="Target">Parenchyma (alveolar region)</td><td data-k="MMAD"><span class="num rv">1 to 3 µm</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td data-k="Target">Parenchyma (listed a second time)</td><td data-k="MMAD"><span class="num rv">&lt;0.1 µm</span></td></tr>
      </table>
      <div class="card warn" style="margin-top:14px">
        <h3>⚠️ Deck quirk — slide 11 lists "Parenchyma" twice</h3>
        <p>The deck gives the alveolar region as <span class="num rv">1–3 µm</span> and then lists "Parenchyma" again at <span class="num rv">&lt;0.1 µm</span>. Both lines are taught here as written — the duplication is in the deck, not in this page. If the exam asks for the alveolar target, answer <span class="num rv">1–3 µm</span>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>⏳ Aging (slide 12)</h3>
      <p><b>Aging</b> is the process by which an aerosol suspension changes over time. How an aerosol ages depends on:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Composition of the aerosol</li>
        <li>Initial size of its particles</li>
        <li>Particle size change due to <b>evaporation</b> or <b>hygroscopic water absorption</b></li>
        <li>Time in suspension</li>
        <li>Ambient conditions</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="anchor"><b>Memory anchor:</b> An aerosol is not frozen in time — it evaporates, absorbs water, and changes size while it hangs in the air.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
<nav class="prevnext" aria-label="Section navigation"><a href="#ch40-deposition">← 3 · Deposition</a><a href="#ch40-measure">5 · Measuring & Hazards →</a></nav></section>

<!-- ============ 5 · MEASURING & HAZARDS ============ -->
<section id="ch40-measure">
  <div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-no">05</div><div>
    <h2>Quantifying delivery — and the hazards of aerosol therapy</h2>
    <p>Two ways to measure what got in, and only one of them tells you where it went.</p>
    <p><span class="chip">Slides 13–14</span> <span class="chip">Also slides 49–50</span></p>
  </div></div>

  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>🔬 Scintigraphy vs. blood/urine assay (slide 13)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Scintigraphy:</b> a drug is "tagged" with a radioactive substance, aerosolized, and inhaled. A scanner measures the distribution and intensity of radiation across the device and the patient's head and thorax. This calculates the percentage of drug retained by the device and delivered to various areas in the patient.</li>
        <li><b>Blood or urine assay over time:</b> does <b>not</b> estimate actual lung delivery — but it gives insight into the systemic drug levels achieved after aerosol administration.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="anchor"><b>Memory anchor:</b> Scintigraphy shows WHERE it went; blood/urine levels only show how much got into the body.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>⚠️ Hazards (slide 14)</h3>
      <p>The <b>primary hazard</b> of aerosol drug therapy is <b>adverse reaction to the medication</b>. Other possible hazards:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Infection</li>
        <li>Secondhand exposure</li>
        <li>Airway reactivity</li>
        <li>Pulmonary and systemic effects</li>
        <li>Eye irritation</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
<nav class="prevnext" aria-label="Section navigation"><a href="#ch40-targets">← 4 · Targets & Aging</a><a href="#ch40-pmdi">6 · pMDI →</a></nav></section>

<!-- ============ 6 · pMDI ============ -->
<section id="ch40-pmdi">
  <div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-no">06</div><div>
    <h2>pMDI: the device, the technique, and the accessories</h2>
    <p>The most commonly prescribed aerosol method — and the one patients most often use wrong.</p>
    <p><span class="chip">Slides 15–23</span></p>
  </div></div>

  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="pop"><figure><img class="tilt" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="pMDI with valved holding chamber"/>
    <figcaption><p class="anchor"><b>Memory anchor:</b> A spacer adds distance, so the fast pMDI spray slows down before it reaches the throat — less oropharyngeal deposition, more drug where it belongs.</p></figcaption></figure></div>
    <div class="stack">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>🧴 The device (slides 15–18)</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>pMDI:</b> a pressurized canister containing the prescribed drug in a volatile propellant combined with <b>surfactant and dispersing agent</b>. The most commonly prescribed method of aerosol therapy: portable, compact, easy to use, multidose convenience.</li>
          <li><b>Serious limitation:</b> it lacks a counter to indicate the number of doses remaining in the canister.</li>
          <li>Most pMDIs are <b>"press and breathe."</b> Variations: <b>breath-actuated pMDIs</b> (trigger activated during inhalation; reduce the need to coordinate actuation with inhalation) and <b>dose counters</b>.</li>
          <li><b>Performance factors:</b> temperature (less serious with newer <b>HFA</b> pMDIs); nozzle size and cleanliness — debris buildup on the nozzle or actuator orifice <b>reduces the emitted dose</b>.</li>
          <li><b>Priming:</b> shaking the device and releasing one or more sprays into the air when the pMDI is new or has not been used for a while. It mixes drug and propellant and is <b>required for an adequate dose</b>.</li>
          <li><b>Timing of actuation intervals:</b> when propellants are released, the device cools and changes aerosol output.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>🎯 Deposition &amp; technique (slides 19–21)</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>pMDIs produce particles in the respirable range (<b>MMAD <span class="num rv">2–6 µm</span></b>).</li>
          <li>About <b><span class="num rv">80%</span> of aerosol deposits in the oropharynx</b>; pulmonary deposition is <b><span class="num rv">10–20%</span></b> in adults and larger children.</li>
          <li>Most patients do <b>not</b> use proper technique. Thorough education can take up to <b><span class="num rv">30 minutes</span></b>.</li>
          <li>The MDI should be actuated at the <b>beginning of inspiration</b> with the mouthpiece held <b><span class="num rv">4 cm</span> in front of the open mouth</b>.</li>
          <li><b>Open-mouth concerns:</b> ipratropium bromide with poor coordination can be sprayed into the eyes; anticholinergic agents have been associated with <b>increased ocular pressure</b>; steroid pMDIs can increase opportunistic oral yeast infection and dysphonia.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>🧩 Accessory devices (slides 22–23)</h3>
        <p>Four basic spacer concepts: (1) small-volume adapters, (2) open-tube designs, (3) bag reservoirs, (4) valved holding chambers. <b>All spacers add distance</b> between the pMDI and the mouth, reducing the initial forward velocity of the droplets.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Spacers:</b> a simple valveless extension device that adds distance; reduces oropharyngeal deposition and the need for hand–breath coordination.</li>
          <li><b>Valved holding chambers:</b> incorporate one or more valves that prevent aerosol in the chamber from being cleared on exhalation; they provide <b>less oropharyngeal deposition, higher respirable drug dosages, and better protection from poor hand–breath coordination</b> than simple spacers.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="anchor"><b>Memory anchor:</b> Spacer = distance. Valved holding chamber = distance PLUS a valve — and that valve is what buys the higher respirable dose.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
