<!-- ============ 7 · SMI & DPI ============ -->
<section id="ch40-smi-dpi">
  <div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-no">07</div><div>
    <h2>Soft mist inhalers &amp; dry powder inhalers</h2>
    <p>One needs no coordination and no spacer. The other needs your lungs to do all the work.</p>
    <p><span class="chip">Slides 24–28</span></p>
  </div></div>

  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="pop"><figure><img class="tilt" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Soft mist inhaler"/>
    <figcaption><p class="anchor"><b>Memory anchor:</b> Soft mist is propellant-free, slower, and longer-lasting — more fine particles, higher lung deposition, lower throat deposition, and no spacer needed.</p></figcaption></figure></div>
    <div class="pop"><figure><img class="tilt" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Dry powder inhaler"/>
    <figcaption><p class="anchor"><b>Memory anchor:</b> No propellant, no hand–breath coordination — your own breath makes the aerosol, but only if you can pull <span class="num rv">40–60 L/min</span>.</p></figcaption></figure></div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:16px">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>🌫️ Soft mist inhalers (slide 24)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><b>SMI:</b> propellant-free, hand-held, liquid inhalers.</li>
        <li>The aerosol cloud contains a <b>higher fraction of fine particles</b>.</li>
        <li>The spray exits <b>more slowly and for a longer duration</b> → <b>higher lung drug deposition and lower oropharyngeal deposition</b>.</li>
        <li><b>Accessory devices such as spacers are not needed.</b></li>
        <li><b>Respimat:</b> an aqueous droplet inhaler (liquid inhaler device).</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>💨 Dry powder inhalers (slides 25–28)</h3>
      <p><b>Breath-actuated</b> dosing system: the patient creates the aerosol by drawing air through a dose of finely milled drug powder. Dispersion into respirable particles depends on creating <b>turbulent flow</b>, which depends on the patient inhaling with a sufficiently high inspiratory flow rate. DPIs do <b>not</b> use propellants and do <b>not</b> require hand–breath coordination.</p>
      <table>
        <tr><th>Category</th><th>Examples</th></tr>
        <tr><td data-k="Category">Unit-dose DPI</td><td data-k="Examples">Aerolizer, Handihaler — individual doses from punctured gelatin capsules</td></tr>
        <tr><td data-k="Category">Multiple-unit dose DPI</td><td data-k="Examples">Diskhaler — case of four or eight individual blister packets on a disk</td></tr>
        <tr><td data-k="Category">Multiple-dose drug-reservoir DPI</td><td data-k="Examples">Twisthaler, Flexhaler, Diskus — preloaded with pure drug for <span class="num rv">120 doses</span></td></tr>
      </table>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Performance factors:</b> intrinsic resistance and inspiratory flow rate; exposure to humidity and moisture; the patient's inspiratory flow ability.</li>
        <li><b>Technique:</b> patients must generate at least <b><span class="num rv">40–60 L/min</span></b> inspiratory flow to produce respirable powder aerosol.</li>
        <li><b>Do NOT use</b> in infants, small children, those who cannot follow instructions, and patients with severe airway obstruction. Clean per the product label.</li>
        <li><b>New DPI technologies:</b> Easyhaler, Ellipta, Podhaler, Tudorza Pressair, Spiromax.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="lab" style="margin-top:16px">
    <h3>🎯 Try it: the device matcher — pick the patient, get the device</h3>
    <p>These are the deck's own selection rules. Tap a patient and the page tells you the device — and the one to avoid.</p>
    <div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Patient scenarios">
      <button type="button" data-dev="coord">Can't coordinate actuation</button>
      <button type="button" data-dev="lowflow">Can't reach 40 L/min</button>
