<!-- ============ 8 · JET NEBULIZERS ============ -->
<section id="ch40-jets">
  <div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-no">08</div><div>
    <h2>Jet nebulizers: SVNs, large-volume devices, and the SPAG</h2>
    <p>The workhorse of the floor — and the device family where a bag or a valve is worth more than a bigger dose.</p>
    <p><span class="chip">Slides 29–34</span></p>
  </div></div>

  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="pop"><figure><img class="tilt" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Jet nebulizer"/>
    <figcaption><p class="anchor"><b>Memory anchor:</b> A jet nebulizer turns liquid into mist with compressed gas — and slow inspiratory flow is what lets the mist settle deep instead of crashing in the throat.</p></figcaption></figure></div>
    <div class="stack">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>🔧 Pneumatic (jet) nebulizers (slide 29)</h3>
        <p>Most are powered by high-pressure oxygen or air from a portable compressor, compressed gas cylinder, or <b><span class="num rv">50-psi</span> wall outlet</b>.</p>
        <p><b>Performance factors:</b> nebulizer design, flow, gas source, density, humidity and temperature, characteristics of the drug formulation.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>🧪 SVN — the four categories (slide 30)</h3>
        <table>
          <tr><th>Category</th><th>What it buys you</th></tr>
          <tr><td data-k="Category">Continuous nebulizer, simple reservoir</td><td data-k="Gain">May increase inhaled dose by <span class="num rv">5–10%</span>, or from <span class="num rv">10% to 11%</span> with a 6-inch piece of reservoir tube</td></tr>
          <tr><td data-k="Category">Continuous nebulizer, collection reservoir bag</td><td data-k="Gain">Bag holds aerosol made during exhalation; small particles stay suspended for the next breath, larger ones rain out — attributed to a <span class="num rv">30–50%</span> increase in inhaled dose</td></tr>
          <tr><td data-k="Category">Breath-enhanced (BE)</td><td data-k="Gain">Generates aerosol continuously, using vents and one-way valves</td></tr>
          <tr><td data-k="Category">Breath-actuated nebulizer (BAN)</td><td data-k="Gain">Can increase inhaled aerosol mass <span class="num rv">3–4×</span> over conventional continuous nebulization</td></tr>
        </table>
        <p class="hint">Deck wording, taught verbatim: the simple-reservoir line reads "increase inhaled dose by 5% to 10%, or increase inhaled dose from 10% to 11% with 6-inch piece of reservoir tube" — the deck gives both phrasings.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>🫁 SVN technique &amp; infection control (slide 31)</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Slow inspiratory flow</b> optimizes SVN aerosol deposition.</li>
          <li>Mask vs. mouthpiece is chosen by patient ability, preference, and comfort.</li>
          <li>Nebulizers should be <b>cleaned and disinfected, or rinsed with sterile water, and air-dried</b> between uses.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>🌀 Large-volume jets &amp; SPAG (slides 32–34)</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Large-volume jet nebulizers also deliver aerosolized drugs; particularly useful when traditional dosing is ineffective in <b>severe bronchospasm</b>. <b>HEART and HOPE</b> = the high-output extended aerosol respiratory therapy nebulizers.</li>
          <li><b>SPAG (small particle aerosol generator):</b> the regulator connects to <b>two flowmeters</b> controlling flow to the nebulizer and flow through the drying chamber. Nebulizer flow ≈ <b><span class="num rv">7 L/min</span></b>; total flow from both flowmeters <b>≥ <span class="num rv">15 L/min</span></b>.</li>
          <li><b>Hand-bulb atomizers and nasal spray pumps:</b> sympathomimetic, anticholinergic, anti-inflammatory, and anesthetic aerosols to the upper airway (nasal passages, pharynx, larynx).</li>
          <li><b>Large-volume USNs:</b> used mainly for bland aerosol therapy or sputum induction.</li>
          <li><b>Vibrating mesh nebulizers</b> (introduced here; detailed in §9).</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="card warn" style="margin-top:14px">
          <h3>⚠️ Deck quirk — slide 33 says "two specific problems" but lists one</h3>
          <p>The SPAG slide announces <b>two specific problems</b> and then lists only one: <b>caregiver exposure</b> to the drug aerosol — which occurs <b>only when the SPAG is used to deliver ribavirin through a mechanical ventilator circuit</b>. Taught as written; there is no second problem on the slide.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
