<!-- ============ 10 · KIDS & PROTOCOLS ============ -->
<section id="ch40-kids">
  <div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-no">10</div><div>
    <h2>Kids, device selection &amp; assessment-based protocols</h2>
    <p>Small airways, fast breathing, and one rule you never break.</p>
    <p><span class="chip">Slides 37–38, 40</span></p>
  </div></div>

  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>👶 Infants and children (slide 37)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Smaller airway diameter than adults</li>
        <li>Breathing rate is faster</li>
        <li>Nose breathing filters out large particles</li>
        <li>Lower minute volumes</li>
        <li>Patient cooperation and ability varies with age and developmental ability</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="card warn" style="margin-top:14px">
        <h3>⛔ Never treat a crying child</h3>
        <p><b>Aerosols should never be administered to a crying child</b> — crying reduces lower-airway deposition of aerosol medication.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>🌬️ Blow-by &amp; selecting a system (slide 38)</h3>
      <p><b>Blow-by technique:</b> used if the patient cannot tolerate mask treatment — the practitioner directs aerosol from the nebulizer toward the patient's nose and mouth from several inches away.</p>
      <p><b>Caution: blow-by may be tolerated but is associated with poor deposition and should be avoided.</b></p>
      <p><b>Selecting a delivery system:</b> it must be prescribed for the appropriate patients and used properly.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>📋 Assessment-based bronchodilator protocols (slide 40)</h3>
      <p><b>Role of the RT:</b> assess patient response. Ongoing patient assessment is key to effective bronchodilator therapy protocol. <b>Peak flow measurement</b> can provide trends if the same device is used from one treatment to the next.</p>
      <p><b>Components of patient assessment:</b> patient interviewing, observation, measurement of vital signs, auscultation, blood gas analysis, oximetry. Conduct <b>dose-response titration</b> to determine the best dosage for patients with moderate obstruction. Patient education.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
<nav class="prevnext" aria-label="Section navigation"><a href="#ch40-smart">← 9 · Smart Nebulizers</a><a href="#ch40-special">11 · Special Circumstances →</a></nav></section>

<!-- ============ 11 · SPECIAL CIRCUMSTANCES ============ -->
<section id="ch40-special">
  <div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-no">11</div><div>
    <h2>Special circumstances — and protecting everyone in the room</h2>
    <p>PAH, off-label use, continuous nebulization, ventilators, NIV, high-flow — and the secondhand aerosol that puts caregivers at risk.</p>
    <p><span class="chip">Slides 41–50</span></p>
  </div></div>

  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="pop"><figure><img class="tilt" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Aerosol delivery during mechanical ventilation"/>
    <figcaption><p class="anchor"><b>Memory anchor:</b> In the ventilator circuit, placement is dose — a pMDI with spacer, USN, or vibrating mesh nebulizer in the inspiratory limb <span class="num rv">0–6 inches</span> from the Y can deliver more than <span class="num rv">15%</span> inhaled dose.</p></figcaption></figure></div>
    <div class="stack">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>🫀 Pulmonary arterial hypertension (slide 41)</h3>
        <p><b>Iloprost</b> and <b>treprostinil</b>, administered with specific nebulizers in discrete doses repeated throughout the day. "Other formulations" are listed with no detail in the deck.</p>
        <h3 style="margin-top:14px">🧪 Acute care &amp; off-label use (slide 42)</h3>
        <p>Clinicians may explore nonstandard methods (doses, frequency, devices) for approved inhaled drugs in acute care, and drugs not approved for inhalation (from heparin to certain antibiotics). <b>Should be avoided when an approved, viable alternative exists.</b> Off-label administration should always be backed by appropriate departmental or institutional policies and procedures.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>⏱️ Continuous nebulization — CBT (slide 43)</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>CBT with nebulized albuterol at <b><span class="num rv">5–20 mg/hour</span></b> proved safe for adult and pediatric patients with severe asthma.</li>
          <li>Assess <b>every <span class="num rv">30 minutes</span> for the first <span class="num rv">2 hours</span>, then hourly</b>. Observe for adverse drug responses.</li>
          <li><b>Positive response:</b> PEFR increase of at least <b><span class="num rv">10%</span> after the first hour</b>.</li>
          <li><b>Goal:</b> at least <b><span class="num rv">50%</span> of predicted value</b>.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>🫁 Ventilator delivery &amp; response (slides 44–46)</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Four primary generator forms</b> during mechanical ventilation: <b>SVN, USN, VM, pMDI with third-party adapter</b>.</li>
          <li><b>Assessing response:</b> measure the change in the difference between <b>peak and plateau pressures</b> — a drop in peak pressure suggests effective bronchodilation. <b>Auto-PEEP</b> levels may decrease in response to bronchodilators. Watch breath-to-breath variations.</li>
          <li><b>Placement (slide 46, verbatim both sentences):</b> during adult ventilation without bias flow, placing a pMDI with spacer, USN, or VMN in the inspiratory limb, <b><span class="num rv">0–6 inches</span> from the circuit Y</b>, can deliver <b>&gt;<span class="num rv">15%</span> inhaled dose</b> in a humidified circuit. In contrast, the deck also states that pMDI, USN, and VMN devices are more efficient when placed close to the patient at the circuit wye — both sentences are taught as written.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>🌬️ NIV, high-flow, IPV, HFOV (slides 47–48)</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><b>NIV:</b> administered with standard and bilevel ventilators. Bilevel ventilators often use a flow turbine with a fixed valve or leak, permitting excess flow to vent to atmosphere. A <b>VM nebulizer delivers a greater inhaled dose than an SVN</b> during NIV.</li>
          <li><b>High-flow nasal oxygen:</b> nebulizer type and location, cannula size, respiratory pattern, and oxygen flow affect inhaled dose. <b>Heliox (<span class="num rv">80:20</span>)</b> improves aerosol delivery at higher flow rates.</li>
          <li><b>IPV (intrapulmonary percussive ventilation):</b> high-frequency oscillation of the airway while administering aerosol; place the generator <b>as close to the patient's airway as possible</b>.</li>
          <li><b>HFOV (high-frequency oscillatory ventilation):</b> albuterol sulfate via VM placed between ventilator circuit and patient airway delivers <b>&gt;<span class="num rv">10%</span> of dose</b> to infants and adults.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card warn">
        <h3>🛡️ Controlling environmental contamination (slides 49–50)</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Nebulized drugs may enter the room directly from the nebulizer or during patient exhalation.</li>
          <li><b>Pentamidine and ribavirin</b> were associated with health risks to caregivers.</li>
          <li><b>Continuous pneumatic nebulizers produce the greatest amount of secondhand aerosol.</b></li>
          <li>Protection: one-way valves and filters help; negative-pressure rooms and treatment booths are useful; <b>PPE is recommended</b> when caring for a patient with a disease that can spread by the airborne route.</li>
          <li>The greatest risk is airborne transmission of <b>tuberculosis, chickenpox, COVID-19, and other highly contagious viruses</b>.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
