<!-- ============ 2 · GAS CHARACTERISTICS ============ -->
<section id="ch41-gases">
  <div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-no">02</div><div>
    <h2>Know each gas by its properties — density, danger, and use</h2>
    <p>Six gases, six property cards. Density relative to air is the thread: it decides whether a gas rises, sinks, or changes how air flows.</p>
    <p><span class="chip">Slides 4, 8–12</span></p>
  </div></div>
  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="card"><h3>Oxygen (O<sub>2</sub>) — slide 4</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Colorless, odorless, transparent, tasteless.</li>
        <li>At STPD, density <span class="num rv">1.429 g/L</span> — slightly heavier than air (<span class="num rv">1.29 g/L</span>).</li>
        <li>Not very soluble in water: at room temperature and 1 atm, only <span class="num rv">3.3 mL</span> of O<sub>2</sub> dissolves in <span class="num rv">100 mL</span> of water.</li>
        <li><b>Nonflammable but greatly accelerates combustion.</b> Burning speed increases with either an increase in O<sub>2</sub> percentage at a fixed total pressure, or an increase in total pressure of O<sub>2</sub> at a constant gas concentration.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="anchor"><b>Memory anchor:</b> Oxygen doesn't burn — it makes everything else burn faster. That one fact explains half the safety rules in this chapter.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Air — slide 8</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Colorless, odorless, naturally occurring gas mixture.</li>
        <li>Contains <span class="num rv">20.95%</span> O<sub>2</sub>, <span class="num rv">78.1%</span> nitrogen, and about <span class="num rv">1%</span> trace gases.</li>
        <li>At STPD, density <span class="num rv">1.29 g/L</span>.</li>
        <li>Medical-grade air is produced by <b>filtering and compressing atmospheric air</b>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="anchor"><b>Memory anchor:</b> Air is the reference gas — 1.29 g/L is the number every other density is judged against.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Carbon dioxide (CO<sub>2</sub>) — slide 9</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>At STPD, colorless and odorless, specific gravity <span class="num rv">1.52</span> — approximately <span class="num rv">1.5 times</span> heavier than air.</li>
        <li><b>Does not support combustion.</b></li>
        <li>Produced by <b>heating limestone in contact with water</b>.</li>
        <li>FDA purity standard: <span class="num rv">99%</span>.</li>
        <li>Common uses for CO<sub>2</sub> mixtures: <b>calibration of blood gas analyzers</b> and <b>diagnostic purposes in the clinical laboratory</b>.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Helium (He) — slide 10</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Odorless, tasteless, nonflammable, chemically and physiologically inert.</li>
        <li>Density <span class="num rv">0.1785 g/L</span> — much less than air.</li>
        <li>Commercially produced from natural gas through liquefaction, to purity standards of at least <span class="num rv">99%</span>.</li>
        <li><b>Must always be mixed with at least <span class="num rv">20%</span> O<sub>2</sub>.</b></li>
        <li>Therapeutic use — <b>heliox</b> (O<sub>2</sub> + helium): manages severe airway obstruction, decreases work of breathing. Lower density makes gas flow <b>more laminar</b>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="anchor"><b>Memory anchor:</b> Helium is the lightest gas in the chapter — light gas, laminar flow, easier breathing through a tight airway.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Nitric oxide (NO) — slide 11</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Colorless, nonflammable, <b>toxic</b> gas that <b>supports combustion</b>.</li>
        <li>Exposure to high concentrations alone can cause <b>methemoglobinemia</b>, which can cause tissue hypoxia.</li>
        <li>FDA approved for <b>term and near-term infants with hypoxic respiratory failure</b>, typically associated with <b>Persistent Pulmonary Hypertension of the Newborn (PPHN)</b>.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Nitrous oxide (N<sub>2</sub>O) — slide 12</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Colorless gas with <b>slightly sweet odor and taste</b>.</li>
        <li>Clinically used as an <b>anesthetic agent</b>. Must always be mixed with oxygen.</li>
        <li>Produced by <b>thermal decomposition of ammonium nitrate</b>.</li>
        <li>Risks of long-term exposure: <b>neuropathy issues, fetal disorders, spontaneous abortion</b>.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="card warn" style="margin-top:16px"><h3>⚠️ Two gases that support combustion — don't confuse them with fuel</h3>
    <p>NO (slide 11) and O<sub>2</sub> (slide 4) both <b>support combustion without being flammable themselves</b>. CO<sub>2</sub> (slide 9) is the opposite: it does <b>not</b> support combustion. Exam questions love this three-way split.</p></div>

<nav class="prevnext" aria-label="Section navigation"><a href="#ch41-intro">← 1 · Introduction</a><a href="#ch41-production">3 · O₂ Production →</a></nav></section>

<!-- ============ 3 · PRODUCTION ============ -->
<section id="ch41-production">
  <div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-no">03</div><div>
    <h2>How oxygen is made — three routes, one workhorse</h2>
    <p>Small quantities come from chemistry or separation; nearly all large quantities come from one process.</p>
    <p><span class="chip">Slides 5–7</span></p>
  </div></div>
  <div class="grid g3">
    <div class="card"><h3>🧪 Chemical methods (small quantities)</h3><p>Electrolysis of water and decomposition of <b>sodium chlorate (NaClO<sub>3</sub>)</b> produce small quantities of O<sub>2</sub> (slide 5).</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>🏭 Fractional distillation (large quantities)</h3><p>The <b>most common and least expensive</b> method; produces most large quantities of O<sub>2</sub> (slides 5–6).</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>🔬 Physical separation (small, concentrated)</h3><p>Small quantities of concentrated O<sub>2</sub> are produced by physically separating O<sub>2</sub> from air (slides 5, 7).</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="card" style="margin-top:16px"><h3>Fractional distillation, step by step (slide 6)</h3>
    <p class="flow"><span class="step">Filter air: remove pollutants, water, CO<sub>2</sub></span><span class="arr">→</span><span class="step">Liquefy by compression + rapid-expansion cooling (Joule–Thompson effect)</span><span class="arr">→</span><span class="step">Heat slowly: nitrogen boils off first</span><span class="arr">→</span><span class="step">O<sub>2</sub> remains</span></p>
    <p style="margin-top:10px">Two physical-separation methods (slide 7): <b>molecular sieves</b> absorb nitrogen, trace gases, and water vapor from air; <b>oxygen concentrators</b> pull ambient air through a semipermeable plastic membrane.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="card warn" style="margin-top:16px"><h3>⚠️ Taught as written — deck typo</h3><p>Slide 6 spells it "leaset expensive." The intended word is <b>least</b> expensive — fractional distillation is the cheapest large-scale route.</p></div>

<nav class="prevnext" aria-label="Section navigation"><a href="#ch41-gases">← 2 · Gas Characteristics</a><a href="#ch41-cylinders">4 · Cylinders →</a></nav></section>

<!-- ============ 4 · CYLINDERS ============ -->
<section id="ch41-cylinders">
  <div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-no">04</div><div>
    <h2>Cylinders — what they are, how they're marked, tested, filled, and emptied</h2>
    <p>Seven slides, one object. If you can read a cylinder's shoulder, test it, fill it, and calculate how long it lasts, you own this section.</p>
    <p><span class="chip">Slides 13–19</span></p>
  </div></div>
  <div class="pop"><figure><img class="tilt" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Color-coded medical gas cylinders with shoulder markings, valves, and caps"/><figcaption>Color-coded cylinders with shoulder stampings, valves, and caps — one chained to the wall, exactly as the storage rules require.<p class="anchor"><b>Memory anchor:</b> The shoulder tells the truth — size, filling pressure, serial number, owner, maker. Color is a hint; the stamping is the identity.</p></figcaption></figure></div>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:16px">
    <div class="card"><h3>What a cylinder is (slide 13)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Used to <b>store and ship compressed or liquid medical gases</b>.</li>
        <li>Design, manufacture, transport, and use are controlled by <b>industrial standards and federal regulations</b>.</li>
        <li>Made of <b>seamless steel</b>, classified by the U.S. Department of Transportation (DOT).</li>
        <li>DOT type <span class="num rv">3A</span>: carbon steel. DOT type <span class="num rv">3AA</span>: steel alloy tempered for higher strength.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Markings and identification (slide 14)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Color coded</b> and marked with <b>metal stamping on the shoulder</b>.</li>
        <li>Stamping indicates: <b>size, normal filling pressure, serial number, ownership, and method of manufacture</b>.</li>
        <li>Safety tests every <span class="num rv">5 or 10 years</span>: cylinders are pressurized to <span class="num rv">five thirds</span> of their service pressure; leakage, expansion, and wall stress are measured; results are stamped on the tank.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Safety relief valves (slide 15)</h3>
      <p>Designed to <b>vent gas to the atmosphere if the tank is heated</b>, preventing tank pressure from becoming too high. Three basic designs:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Frangible metal disk</b> — ruptures at a specific pressure.</li>
        <li><b>Fusible plug</b> — melts at a specific temperature.</li>
        <li><b>Spring-loaded valve</b> — opens and vents gas at a set high pressure.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Filling / charging (slide 16)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Compressed gases:</b> normally filled to their <b>service pressure</b> (the pressure stamped on the shoulder) at <span class="num rv">70°F</span>. Approved cylinders can be filled to <span class="num rv">10%</span> in excess of service pressure.</li>
        <li><b>Liquefied gases</b> (CO<sub>2</sub> and N<sub>2</sub>O): filled according to <b>specified filling density</b> — the ratio between the weight of liquid gas put into the cylinder and the weight of water the cylinder would contain if full.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Measuring cylinder contents (slide 17)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Gas-filled cylinders:</b> volume of gas is <b>directly proportional to its pressure</b> — the gauge tells you the content.</li>
        <li><b>Liquid-gas cylinders:</b> pressure does <b>not</b> relate to the amount of liquid remaining. <b>Only the weight of the cylinder indicates the amount of gas inside.</b></li>
      </ul>
      <p class="anchor"><b>Memory anchor:</b> Gas: trust the gauge. Liquid: trust the scale. Mixing those up is the classic cylinder question.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Duration of flow — the formulas (slides 18–19)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Factors affecting duration: <b>gas flow, cylinder size, cylinder pressure at start of therapy</b>.</li>
        <li>Cylinder factor (L/psig) = <b>cubic feet (full cylinder) × <span class="num rv">28.3</span> ÷ pressure (full cylinder) in psig</b>.</li>
        <li>Duration of flow = <b>content ÷ flow</b>; Duration (min) = <b>pressure (psig) × cylinder factor ÷ flow (L/min)</b>.</li>
        <li>Liquid O<sub>2</sub>: <b>1 L of liquid O<sub>2</sub> weighs <span class="num rv">2.5 lb</span> and produces <span class="num rv">860 L</span></b> of gaseous O<sub>2</sub>. Amount of gas = liquid O<sub>2</sub> weight (lb) × 860 ÷ 2.5. Duration (min) = amount of gas (L) ÷ flow (L/min).</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="card goldbox" style="margin-top:16px"><h3>🧮 Worked example — liquid oxygen (deck constants only)</h3>
    <p>A container holds <b>10 lb</b> of liquid O<sub>2</sub>, running at <b>2 L/min</b>. Amount of gas = 10 × 860 ÷ 2.5 = <b>3,440 L</b>. Duration = 3,440 ÷ 2 = <b>1,720 minutes</b> (about 28.7 hours). Try your own numbers in the calculator below — the formulas are the deck's, the inputs are yours.</p></div>
  <div class="lab" style="margin-top:16px">
    <h3>🧮 Try it — cylinder duration calculator (deck formulas)</h3>
    <div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Calculator mode">
      <button type="button" data-calc="compressed" aria-pressed="true">Compressed cylinder</button>
      <button type="button" data-calc="liquid" aria-pressed="false">Liquid O₂ cylinder</button>
    </div>
    <div id="ch41-calcCompressed">
      <p>Duration (min) = pressure (psig) × cylinder factor (L/psig) ÷ flow (L/min)</p>
      <div class="grid g3">
        <label>Pressure (psig)<br/><input id="ch41-inP" type="number" value="1000" min="0" style="width:100%;padding:9px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;font:inherit"/></label>
        <label>Cylinder factor (L/psig)<br/><input id="ch41-inF" type="number" value="0.16" min="0" step="0.01" style="width:100%;padding:9px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;font:inherit"/></label>
        <label>Flow (L/min)<br/><input id="ch41-inQ" type="number" value="2" min="0.1" step="0.1" style="width:100%;padding:9px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;font:inherit"/></label>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div id="ch41-calcLiquid" style="display:none">
      <p>Amount of gas (L) = weight (lb) × 860 ÷ 2.5 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Duration (min) = amount ÷ flow</p>
      <div class="grid g2">
        <label>Liquid O₂ weight (lb)<br/><input id="ch41-inW" type="number" value="10" min="0" step="0.1" style="width:100%;padding:9px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;font:inherit"/></label>
        <label>Flow (L/min)<br/><input id="ch41-inQ2" type="number" value="2" min="0.1" step="0.1" style="width:100%;padding:9px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;font:inherit"/></label>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="score" id="ch41-calcOut" style="margin-top:12px"></p>
    <p class="hint">Cylinder factor itself = cubic feet (full cylinder) × 28.3 ÷ pressure (full cylinder) in psig (slide 18). For a liquid cylinder, never use the pressure gauge — weigh it (slide 17).</p>
  </div>
  <div class="card warn" style="margin-top:16px"><h3>⚠️ Taught as written — formula wording</h3><p>Slide 18 writes the cylinder-factor formula as "cubic feet × 28.3 / pressure," and slide 37 writes the restrictor relation as "R = P1 – P2 / V rearranged to V = P1 – P2 / R." Read with standard grouping: <b>V = (P1 − P2) ÷ R</b>. Slide 19's liquid-oxygen amount is written as "weight (lb) × 860/2.5 lb/L" — the 860 L of gas per 2.5 lb is the deck's conversion, used exactly that way in the calculator above.</p></div>

