<!-- ============ 5 · HANDLING ============ -->
<section id="ch41-handling">
  <div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-no">05</div><div>
    <h2>Store it, move it, use it — without turning a tank into a hazard</h2>
    <p>Seven slides of rules. They read like common sense until you notice how many of them exist because someone once did the opposite.</p>
    <p><span class="chip">Slides 20–26</span></p>
  </div></div>
  <div class="pop"><figure><img class="tilt" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Ventilated gas storage enclosure with chained, segregated cylinders"/><figcaption>A ventilated enclosure with chained, segregated cylinders and a caution sign on the door — the storage slides, built.<p class="anchor"><b>Memory anchor:</b> Chained, capped, separated, signed, cool, ventilated. Miss any one and the storage rule fails.</p></figcaption></figure></div>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:16px">
    <div class="card"><h3>Storage (slides 20–22)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Store cylinders in <b>racks or chained to the wall</b> so they cannot fall or be damaged.</li>
        <li>Store <b>away from combustible material</b> and <b>away from heat</b>; keep cylinder temperature <b>below <span class="num rv">125°F</span> (&lt;<span class="num rv">52°C</span>)</b>.</li>
        <li>Store <b>flammable gases separately</b> from gases that support combustion (air, O<sub>2</sub>, N<sub>2</sub>O).</li>
        <li>Cap on when not in use. <b>Do not store air compressors and gas cylinders together</b> — a fire involving one can damage both delivery systems.</li>
        <li>Supply systems live in an enclosure that is <b>well ventilated and drained</b>, built of material with at least a <span class="num rv">1-hour</span> fire-resistive rating.</li>
        <li><b>Segregate full and empty cylinders</b>; store them separately if possible.</li>
        <li>Sign on each door/gate warning of oxidizing gas and cautioning against smoking — <b>readable from at least <span class="num rv">5 ft</span> (<span class="num rv">1.5 m</span>)</b>.</li>
        <li>Store liquid O<sub>2</sub> containers in a <b>cool, well-ventilated area</b>: small amounts of O<sub>2</sub> continuously vent from these low-pressure containers, and that venting <b>prevents over-pressurization</b> as liquid O<sub>2</sub> keeps converting to gas.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Transport (slide 23)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Use <b>cylinder carts with a securing mechanism</b>.</li>
        <li>Keep <b>protective caps in place</b> during transport.</li>
        <li>Protect cylinders from <b>striking other cylinders or objects</b> — damage to safety devices, valve stems, or the cylinder itself.</li>
        <li><b>Avoid dropping, dragging, or rolling</b> cylinders in transport.</li>
        <li><b>Do not transport cylinders that are not appropriately labeled.</b></li>
      </ul>
      <h3 style="margin-top:14px">Use at the bedside (slides 24–26)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Secure the cylinder</b> so it cannot fall: chain to the wall, bind/chain to a suitable cart, or support with a stand. Never secure to a movable object unless it has an apparatus that can contain the cylinder safely.</li>
        <li><b>No flammable materials — especially oil or grease</b> — on regulators, cylinders, fittings, or valves. That includes oily hands, rags, and gloves.</li>
        <li><b>"Crack" the cylinder before bringing it to the bedside:</b> open the valve slightly to remove dust and dirt before attaching the regulator — and make sure <b>no one is in front of it</b> while you do.</li>
        <li>Never use cylinder valves or regulators that <b>need repair</b>. Do not alter or deface cylinder markings or color. Never place cylinders near sources of heat.</li>
        <li>Regulator–valve connection: <b>American Standard Safety System (ASSS) for H and G cylinders; pin-index safety system (PISS) for E cylinders</b>.</li>
        <li>When O<sub>2</sub> is in use, post a <b>"No Smoking" sign</b> unless entrance signs already prohibit smoking in the facility.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

<nav class="prevnext" aria-label="Section navigation"><a href="#ch41-cylinders">← 4 · Cylinders</a><a href="#ch41-bulk">6 · Bulk Oxygen →</a></nav></section>

<!-- ============ 6 · BULK ============ -->
<section id="ch41-bulk">
  <div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-no">06</div><div>
    <h2>Bulk oxygen — how a whole hospital breathes without a thousand tanks</h2>
    <p>Portable cylinders don't scale. Bulk systems do — and when one fails, the failure plan is the lesson.</p>
    <p><span class="chip">Slides 27–30</span></p>
  </div></div>
  <div class="pop"><figure><img class="tilt" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Bulk liquid oxygen tank with vaporizer coils and manifolded reserve cylinders beside a hospital"/><figcaption>Bulk liquid tank, vaporizer coils, manifolded reserve bank, and a smaller backup tank — the whole supply chain in one yard.<p class="anchor"><b>Memory anchor:</b> Bulk = one big tank instead of a thousand small ones. Small liquid volume, enormous gas volume, one point of failure — so one backup is mandatory.</p></figcaption></figure></div>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:16px">
    <div class="card"><h3>Bulk oxygen (slide 27)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Used to meet <b>large O<sub>2</sub> needs</b> of health care facilities. Systems hold at least <span class="num rv">20,000 cubic feet</span> of gas. O<sub>2</sub> may be stored in gas or liquid form.</li>
        <li>Advantages over portable cylinders: <b>far less expensive over the long term; less prone to interruption; eliminates the inconvenience and hazard of transporting and storing large numbers of cylinders; eliminates the need for separate pressure-reducing valves at each outlet; safer because they operate at low pressures</b>.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card"><h3>The three supply systems (slides 28–29)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Alternating supply / cylinder manifold system:</b> large (normally H or K size) cylinders of compressed O<sub>2</sub> banked together in series. When pressure in the primary bank decreases to a set level, the control valve <b>automatically switches to the reserve bank</b>.</li>
        <li><b>Cylinder supply system with reserve supply:</b> primary, secondary, and reserve supplies. When the primary is depleted by demand, the system <b>automatically switches to the secondary</b>.</li>
        <li><b>Bulk gas system with reserve:</b> most commonly used in large health facilities for economic, safety, and convenience reasons. A <b>small volume of liquid O<sub>2</sub> provides a very large amount of gaseous O<sub>2</sub></b> and minimizes space requirements.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="card warn" style="margin-top:16px"><h3>⚠️ If bulk oxygen fails (slide 30) — this is the must-know</h3>
    <p>Failure of a bulk O<sub>2</sub> supply can be <b>life threatening</b> to any patient receiving O<sub>2</sub> or gas-powered ventilatory support. Facilities should have a <b>second, smaller liquid stand tank or cylinder gas manifold as backup</b>. Staff must be prepared to <b>identify affected patients and move appropriate backup equipment to the bedside</b> — portable cylinders, bag-mask resuscitators, and the like.</p></div>
  <div class="card" style="margin-top:16px"><h3>⚠️ Taught as written — the "three types" span two slides</h3><p>Slide 29 is headed "Three Types" but lists only two systems on that slide (cylinder supply with reserve; bulk gas with reserve). The third — the alternating/manifold system — is on slide 28. Counted across both slides, the three are: <b>alternating/manifold, cylinder supply with reserve, bulk gas with reserve</b>.</p></div>

<nav class="prevnext" aria-label="Section navigation"><a href="#ch41-handling">← 5 · Storage, Transport & Use</a><a href="#ch41-distribution">7 · Distribution & Connectors →</a></nav></section>

<!-- ============ 7 · DISTRIBUTION ============ -->
<section id="ch41-distribution">
  <div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-no">07</div><div>
    <h2>From the tank to the wall outlet — pressure down, safety built into every connection</h2>
    <p>Distribution has one job (usable pressure at the bedside) and connectors have one job (the wrong gas must not fit).</p>
    <p><span class="chip">Slides 31–34</span></p>
  </div></div>
  <div class="grid g2">
    <div class="card"><h3>Distribution and regulation (slide 31)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Primary function: <b>deliver O<sub>2</sub>/air to the bedside at usable pressure</b>.</li>
        <li>Central piping system delivers compressed gas to all areas throughout the hospital.</li>
        <li>Gas pressure is reduced to a standard working pressure of <span class="num rv">50 psi</span> at the bulk storage location.</li>
        <li>Main alarm warns of <b>pressure drops or interruptions in flow</b> from the source.</li>
        <li>Zone valves are located throughout the system — <b>closed for maintenance or in case of fire</b>.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Regulating pressure and flow (slide 34)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>A <b>high-pressure reducing valve</b> reduces gas pressure to a usable level: single-stage or multiple-stage; preset or adjustable.</li>
        <li>A <b>flowmeter</b> controls flow to the patient.</li>
        <li>A <b>regulator</b> controls <b>both pressure and flow</b>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="anchor"><b>Memory anchor:</b> Valve = pressure down. Flowmeter = flow set. Regulator = both jobs in one device.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Safety indexed connector systems (slide 32)</h3>
      <p>Three systems ensure the correct gas source:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><b>American Standard Safety System (ASSS)</b> — for large cylinders and their attachments; prevents accidental misconnections.</li>
        <li><b>Pin-Index Safety System (PISS)</b> — for small cylinders (up to and including size E) and their attachments; yoke-type connection; exact pin/pinhole positions vary for each gas: <span class="num rv">2-5</span> for oxygen, <span class="num rv">1-5</span> for air.</li>
        <li><b>Diameter-Index Safety System (DISS)</b> — for low-pressure gas connectors; found at outlets of pressure-reducing valves, outlets of central piping systems, and inlets of blenders, flowmeters, and ventilators.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="hint">Note the two wordings, both in the deck: slide 26 says ASSS for H and G cylinders and PISS for E cylinders; slide 32 says ASSS for large cylinders and PISS for small cylinders up to and including size E. Same rule, stated both ways.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Quick connect systems (slide 33)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Station outlets at the bedside allow quick access to a bulk supply of <b>O<sub>2</sub>, air, or vacuum</b>.</li>
        <li>Outlets have <b>DISS connections or quick-connect systems</b> that are gas-specific or vacuum-specific.</li>
        <li>Manufacturers designed <b>specially shaped connectors for each gas</b>: each connector has a distinct shape, <b>does not fit into an outlet for another gas</b>, and each manufacturer has its own unique design.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

<nav class="prevnext" aria-label="Section navigation"><a href="#ch41-bulk">← 6 · Bulk Oxygen</a><a href="#ch41-flowmeters">8 · Flowmeters →</a></nav></section>

<!-- ============ 8 · FLOWMETERS ============ -->
<section id="ch41-flowmeters">
  <div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-no">08</div><div>
    <h2>Flowmeters — the Thorpe tube, the Bourdon gauge, and knowing which one you're reading</h2>
    <p>The heart of the chapter's bedside skill: three meter types, one comparison that decides transport, accuracy, and truth.</p>
    <p><span class="chip">Slides 35–41</span></p>
  </div></div>
  <div class="pop"><figure><img class="tilt" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="Thorpe tube flowmeter beside a Bourdon gauge regulator on a cylinder"/><figcaption>Left: the Thorpe tube — tall, tapered, gravity-dependent, read at the wall. Right: the Bourdon gauge — dial, fixed orifice, built for transport.<p class="anchor"><b>Memory anchor:</b> Wall = Thorpe (upright, true flow). Transport = Bourdon (any angle, but pressure-dependent). If the question mentions an ambulance or a hallway, it has already told you the answer.</p></figcaption></figure></div>
  <div class="card" style="margin-top:16px"><h3>The three categories (slide 35)</h3>
    <p>Flowmeters let the rate of gas flow to the patient be <b>set and controlled</b>. Three categories are used: <b>flow restrictor, Bourdon gauge, Thorpe tube</b>.</p></div>
  <table style="margin-top:16px">
    <tr><th>Device</th><th>How it works</th><th>Key traits — exam gold</th></tr>
    <tr><td data-k="Device"><b>Flow restrictor</b> (slides 36–37)</td><td data-k="How it works">Simplest and least expensive. Fixed orifice calibrated to deliver a specific flow at constant pressure; operation is based on flow resistance. V = (P1 − P2) ÷ R, where V = volumetric flow per unit time, P1 = upstream pressure, P2 = downstream pressure, R = total resistance.</td><td data-k="Key traits">Fixed orifice; constant pressure; cheapest and simplest.</td></tr>
    <tr><td data-k="Device"><b>Bourdon gauge</b> (slide 38)</td><td data-k="How it works">Always used in combination with an adjustable pressure-reducing valve. Uses a fixed orifice but operates under variable pressures.</td><td data-k="Key traits"><b>Not gravity dependent — ideal for patient transport.</b> Not accurate when pressure distal to the orifice changes.</td></tr>
    <tr><td data-k="Device"><b>Integrated oxygen cylinders</b> (slide 39)</td><td data-k="How it works">Eliminates the need for separate oxygen tanks, Bourdon gauge regulators, and oxygen keys/wrenches. Select the flow and connect oxygen tubing to the system and to the patient.</td><td data-k="Key traits">Alerts activate when cylinder duration falls below <span class="num rv">15 minutes</span>.</td></tr>
    <tr><td data-k="Device"><b>Thorpe tube</b> (slides 40–41)</td><td data-k="How it works">Variable-orifice, constant-pressure device. Increasing the size of the orifice increases gas flow. Always attached to a <span class="num rv">50-psig</span> source. Used to measure true flow.</td><td data-k="Key traits"><b>Gravity dependent — not ideal for patient transport.</b> Two types below.</td></tr>
  </table>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:16px">
    <div class="card"><h3>Thorpe: pressure-compensated (slide 41)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Prevents changes in downstream resistance, or back pressure, from affecting meter accuracy.</b></li>
        <li>Calibrated at <span class="num rv">50 psig</span> instead of at atmospheric pressure.</li>
        <li>Flow-control needle valve placed <b>after (distal to)</b> the flow tube.</li>
        <li><b>Gravity dependent; not ideal for patient transport.</b></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="card"><h3>Thorpe: uncompensated (slide 41)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Calibrated in liters per minute at <b>atmospheric pressure</b> (without restriction).</li>
        <li>Gas from the <span class="num rv">50-psig</span> source flows into the meter at a rate controlled by a needle valve located <b>before</b> the flow tube.</li>
        <li>Because only the compensated type is stated to prevent back-pressure effects on accuracy, an uncompensated meter's reading <b>is</b> affected when downstream resistance changes — that contrast is the point of the two types.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="lab" style="margin-top:16px">
    <h3>🧭 Try it — flowmeter matcher (deck rules only)</h3>
    <p>Pick a situation; the deck's rule picks the device.</p>
    <div class="seg" role="group" aria-label="Flowmeter scenarios">
      <button type="button" data-fm="transport" aria-pressed="false">Transport patient</button>
      <button type="button" data-fm="backpressure" aria-pressed="false">Back pressure varies</button>
      <button type="button" data-fm="cheapest" aria-pressed="false">Cheapest fixed flow</button>
      <button type="button" data-fm="alert" aria-pressed="false">Warns under 15 min</button>
      <button type="button" data-fm="trueflow" aria-pressed="false">True flow at the wall</button>
    </div>
    <h3 id="ch41-fmTitle" style="margin-top:12px">Pick a situation above.</h3>
    <p id="ch41-fmText" class="hint">Each answer quotes the deck's own trait — gravity, back pressure, cost, alert, or true flow.</p>
  </div>

