  <ol>
    <li>An E cylinder contains 1,250 psig and delivers oxygen at 4 L/min. Calculate the duration.
      <details class="worked"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p>Duration = pressure &times; factor &divide; flow. E-cylinder factor = 0.28 L/psig.<br/>1,250 &times; 0.28 &divide; 4 = 350 &divide; 4 = <b>87.5 minutes (about 88 minutes &mdash; 1 hour 28 minutes)</b>.</p></details></li>
    <li>An H cylinder contains 1,800 psig and delivers oxygen at 8 L/min. Calculate the duration.
      <details class="worked"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p>Duration = pressure &times; factor &divide; flow. H-cylinder factor = 3.14 L/psig.<br/>1,800 &times; 3.14 &divide; 8 = 5,652 &divide; 8 = <b>706.5 minutes (about 707 minutes &mdash; 11 hours 47 minutes)</b>.</p></details></li>
    <li>A patient requires 12 L/min of oxygen during a 70-minute transport. Determine the number of full E cylinders needed, including a safety reserve.
      <details class="worked"><summary>Show worked solution</summary><p>Oxygen needed = 12 &times; 70 = <b>840 L</b>. One full E cylinder (2,200 psig &times; 0.28) holds <b>616 L</b>. 840 &divide; 616 = 1.36, so one cylinder is not enough &rarr; <b>2 full E cylinders</b>. Check with the safety reserve held back: (2,200 &minus; 200) &times; 0.28 = 560 L usable per cylinder, so two give 1,120 L &mdash; still covers 840 L. <b>Answer: 2 full E cylinders.</b></p></details></li>
